# XSH atomic_fetch_add(), _sub, _and, _or, _xor and their _explicit forms
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The ten spellings share one page (the other nine page files are copies).
# Integer tests run on every atomic integer type but atomic_bool, which
# the page excludes.

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.001
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_fetch_add() adds the operand to the object and returns the
           value held before, for every atomic integer type (20 + 7 gives 27
           with 20 returned; adding 0; 27 + 100).
test       t/001-add.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.002
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_fetch_sub() subtracts the operand from the object and
           returns the value held before, for every atomic integer type
           (127 - 7, - 0, - 120).
test       t/002-sub.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.003
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_fetch_and(), atomic_fetch_or() and atomic_fetch_xor() apply
           bitwise AND, inclusive OR and exclusive OR of the operand and
           return the value held before, for every atomic integer type
           (0x55 with 0x33 gives 0x11, 0x77, 0x66).
test       t/003-bitwise.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.004
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       For unsigned atomic integer types addition and subtraction wrap
           around: max + 1 gives 0, 0 - 1 gives max, max + max gives max - 1,
           1 - 2 gives max.
note       Signed types are skipped in this test (XSH.atomic_fetch_add.005);
           fewer than four unsigned types found is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/004-wrap-unsigned.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.005
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       For signed atomic integer types the computation wraps silently on
           overflow: max + 1 gives min, min - 1 gives max, max + max gives
           -2; negative operands work (5 + -8 gives -3, -3 - -4 gives 1).
note       Two's complement is taken from the page's silent wrap-around.
test       t/005-wrap-signed.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.006
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      disputed
since      2024
says       atomic_fetch_add() and atomic_fetch_sub() on atomic pointers
           (to char, short, int, long, double and a 24-byte structure) move
           the pointer by whole objects, return the previous pointer, and
           accept negative and zero operands; the _explicit forms likewise.
note       disputed (Q-156): that the pointer moves in units of the pointed-to
           type is read from APPLICATION USAGE (nearly equivalent to += and
           -=) and from the operand type ptrdiff_t.
test       t/006-pointer-arith.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.007
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       atomic_fetch_add_explicit(), _sub_explicit, _and_explicit,
           _or_explicit and _xor_explicit with each of the six memory orders
           give the same result and return value as the plain forms, for
           every atomic integer type.
test       t/007-explicit-orders.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.008
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.15.1
class      required
since      2024
says       Four threads each calling atomic_fetch_add() 10000 times on an
           atomic int (and on an atomic long long, adding 2): the object
           ends at exactly 40000 (80000), and every previous value 0 to
           39999 is returned exactly once; the same for atomic_fetch_sub()
           counting back to 0 (plain and _explicit forms mixed).
test       t/008-add-sub-contention.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.009
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.15.1
class      required
since      2024
says       Three threads calling atomic_fetch_xor() 10001 times on a shared
           bit and each on a private bit: each thread finds its private bit
           alternating as it left it, and the final value is exactly 0xf.
test       t/009-xor-parity.c

assertion  XSH.atomic_fetch_add.010
anchor     functions/atomic_fetch_add.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.15.1
class      required
since      2024
says       Four threads setting (atomic_fetch_or) and clearing
           (atomic_fetch_and) their own bit of one atomic unsigned int 20000
           times always find the bit clear before setting and set before
           clearing, and the object ends at 0.
test       t/010-or-and-ownership.c
